Z||000004||Voytek||10-03-2001||05:48 PM||||I don't know about new Mitsubishis but just a few years ago they were sh*t.  Think about it.  All Hyundais from late 80's to early 90's used Mitsubishi engines.  Need I say more?<P>Ok.  I'll say more.  My parents have owned a couple of Chryslers - '85 New Yorker and '90 Dynasty.  Both Mitsubishi engines.  PIECES OF CRAP!! Non-stop fuel problems.<P>Here's another one.  A friend owned one of the first Eagle Talons (Eclipse).  What a piece!  Went through 2 engines and 3 transmissions.  The 2nd engine wasn't any better than the first one.  Constant problems.<P>I'd be very cautious before I'd buy ANY Mitsubishi product.||||reg||1
Z||000005||frontal lobe||10-03-2001||10:58 PM||||Chrysler and Mitsubishi did a bunch of joint-venture stuff for a while.  I can't remember the history of it all, but there was AMC, and then Jeep bought them or something, and then it was Jeep-Eagle, and then Chrysler bought them and Jeep dealers needed some cars on their lots so they made a version of the joint venture and called it Eagle.<P>Anyway, Eagle is just another body version of the Chrysler-Mitsubishi collaboration that, as others have said, is mostly Mitsubishi.  Those are actually produced in Normal, Illinois.<P>For example, the Mitsubishi Eclipse was the Chrysler Laser was the Eagle Talon.<P>Kind of like GM often does with Buick/Pontiac/Chevrolet-same platform and engines and division gets to put their little particular styling and spin on it.<P>I can't remember what year they decided to stop making an "Eagle" version, but the division is gone somewhat recently-late '90's.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cooter||10-03-2001||11:05 PM||||The local Mitsu dealer just gave away a 25 mile Monterro (sp) to a trade school. The reason for giving it away? It died on the owner and the ECM had to be replaced- several thousand $$$ worth of work. Then other electronic modules began to fail, and several more thousand $$$$ of repairs. The real problem was that Mitsu would not honor their new car warantee because the dealership improperly installed the trailer hitch and shorted the battery. After that, it would die for no reason and then need more electronics replaced. Each time, it costed the dealer. Soon, they had almost half the value of the NEW truck tied up in repairs. So, to save face to the owner they let him pick another one off the lot and gave his to the trade school- and they still can't get it to run! At the same dealership, they have a 2000 Eclipse that has had 2 transmissions, 3 sunroof assemblies (and the roof painted 3 times because of damaging the paint installing the roof) 2 sets of front seats and 2 complete carpet sets. There is a huge water leak in the sunroof, but it can't be found. So the carpet and seats have already rotted twice. It was a lemon from the first day, but Mitsu will not offer any assistance to the owner. With that kind of dedication to customer service, it would be hard for me to look at buying anything they make.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fformula88||10-04-2001||10:08 AM||||Actually Eagle was around before Chrysler... but as a model, not a make.  The AMC Eagle was a car.  After Chrysler bought out AMC they decided to name the new division Eagle.<P>Also not all cars sold as Eagles were re-badged Mitsubishi's.  There was the Eagle Vision sedan which was a rebadged Dodge Intrepid.  Also the Eagle Premier which was a re-badged Dodge Monico (could this have been a Mitsu?  Was a horrible peace of garbage!).  It didn't have any unique cars at the time they killed it.<P>Mitsu reliability?  Not too good as mentioned.  Just take a look at the reliability ratings for used Eclipses and such.  They are lower than most cars in their class.  Dodge dumped the Avenger name because of the bad image poor reliability had earned that car.  It used Mitsubishi drivetrains too.  They may be Japanese, but they certainly are not Honda or Toyota in quality!||||reg||1
